01:27:13.350 --> 01:27:18.350 line:15%
and strain that your life delivers to you on a daily basis.

2068
01:27:18.540 --> 01:27:21.700
It's gotta be a lot to just shoulder everything

2069
01:27:21.700 --> 01:27:23.110
that this city throws at you.

2070
01:27:23.110 --> 01:27:24.780
So how do you, like,

2071
01:27:24.780 --> 01:27:27.860
what is your routine to just maintain your equanimity

2072
01:27:27.860 --> 01:27:29.033
throughout everything?

2073
01:27:30.490 --> 01:27:32.470
It is heavy, there's no doubt about it.

2074
01:27:32.470 --> 01:27:36.350
And it's both not about me,

2075
01:27:36.350 --> 01:27:38.090
which I fully recognize.

2076
01:27:38.090 --> 01:27:42.630
I mean this hundred year in the making reckoning

2077
01:27:42.630 --> 01:27:46.390
around racial justice is not about me specifically as mayor,

2078
01:27:46.390 --> 01:27:49.670
but obviously I'm very involved

2079
01:27:49.670 --> 01:27:51.050
as the mayor of Minneapolis.

2080
01:27:51.050 --> 01:27:53.637
And you feel that weight every single day,

2081
01:27:55.770 --> 01:27:57.160
I have a little bit of a mantra

2082
01:27:57.160 --> 01:27:59.633
that I tell myself every morning when I wake up,

2083
01:28:01.670 --> 01:28:02.710
the first thing I tell myself

2084
01:28:02.710 --> 01:28:04.830
is find a way that's always been my mantra.

2085
01:28:04.830 --> 01:28:06.330
I have find a way for whatever it is

2086
01:28:06.330 --> 01:28:07.400
that you're trying to figure out,

2087
01:28:07.400 --> 01:28:09.740
whatever problem you're trying to solve,

2088
01:28:09.740 --> 01:28:12.950
whatever form of justice

2089
01:28:12.950 --> 01:28:15.663
or equity you're trying to accomplish, it's find a way.

2090
01:28:16.510 --> 01:28:18.400
But then there's a few simple principles

2091
01:28:18.400 --> 01:28:19.630
that I live by beyond that.

2092
01:28:19.630 --> 01:28:20.860
And sometimes when you're going through

2093
01:28:20.860 --> 01:28:25.420
the most strenuous difficulty and that's all you can do,

2094
01:28:25.420 --> 01:28:27.960
it's first tell the truth,

2095
01:28:27.960 --> 01:28:29.603
be honest and no matter what,

2096
01:28:30.560 --> 01:28:32.550
second you try to do what's right.

2097
01:28:32.550 --> 01:28:34.890
And obviously what is right

2098
01:28:34.890 --> 01:28:36.533
is oftentimes up for debate.

2099
01:28:37.626 --> 01:28:39.440
And then the last thing is you just try your hardest

2100
01:28:39.440 --> 01:28:41.740
and you keep putting one foot in front of the other

2101
01:28:41.740 --> 01:28:43.810
and that's all you can do.

2102
01:28:43.810 --> 01:28:44.880
So it's that mantra

2103
01:28:44.880 --> 01:28:46.810
and then of course it's running.

2104
01:28:46.810 --> 01:28:48.960
I mean, running has always been

2105
01:28:48.960 --> 01:28:51.500
some form of release for me

2106
01:28:51.500 --> 01:28:54.360
and has become more of a release

2107
01:28:54.360 --> 01:28:56.283
since I stopped doing it seriously.

2108
01:28:57.180 --> 01:28:58.380
Yeah I want to talk about the running.

2109
01:28:58.380 --> 01:29:00.930
Let's just get into that now.

2110
01:29:00.930 --> 01:29:03.640
I can't imagine there's anybody

2111
01:29:03.640 --> 01:29:06.070
in elected office in the U.S

2112
01:29:06.070 --> 01:29:09.340
who's a more accomplished runner than yourself.

2113
01:29:09.340 --> 01:29:11.010
I think you have that crown don't you?

2114
01:29:11.010 --> 01:29:12.410
two 16 marathoner.

2115
01:29:12.410 --> 01:29:14.150
I mean, it's unbelievable.

2116
01:29:14.150 --> 01:29:16.210
Yeah I don't know it's possible.

2117
01:29:16.210 --> 01:29:18.220
I feel like lately any way,

2118
01:29:18.220 --> 01:29:20.600
runners have been getting more and more involved

2119
01:29:20.600 --> 01:29:23.990
in different causes, social justice, politics, et cetera,

2120
01:29:23.990 --> 01:29:25.130
who knows?

2121
01:29:25.130 --> 01:29:27.193
That was certainly my previous life.

2122
01:29:28.360 --> 01:29:33.100
Pro runner and was competing as a pro all the way

2123
01:29:33.100 --> 01:29:35.450
through law school or part of law school.

2124
01:29:35.450 --> 01:29:38.950
Yeah I, well, so I graduated college

2125
01:29:38.950 --> 01:29:42.600
I very soon thereafter moved out to join

2126
01:29:42.600 --> 01:29:46.450
the Hanson's Olympic Development Program with Brooks.

2127
01:29:46.450 --> 01:29:49.315
(overlapping conversation)

2128
01:29:49.315 --> 01:29:50.148
She was Desi Davila at the time.

2129
01:29:50.148 --> 01:29:52.950
And then she married our buddy Ryan

2130
01:29:54.083 --> 01:29:59.083
and it was a wonderful experience out in the Hanson's.

2131
01:29:59.280 --> 01:30:01.830
They've got this amazing program where,

2132
01:30:01.830 --> 01:30:04.990
you just get up and you start running

2133
01:30:04.990 --> 01:30:06.410
with a big group of people

2134
01:30:06.410 --> 01:30:08.400
that are doing exactly the same thing.

2135
01:30:08.400 --> 01:30:11.230
And it becomes a whole lot easier to run very high mileage

2136
01:30:11.230 --> 01:30:12.690
because you have that kind

2137
01:30:12.690 --> 01:30:14.523
of camaraderie associated with it.

2138
01:30:15.380 --> 01:30:17.910
So I did that for a year and a half or so,

2139
01:30:17.910 --> 01:30:19.590
and then went back and probably

2140
01:30:19.590 --> 01:30:22.190
had my very best running years of my entire career

2141
01:30:22.190 --> 01:30:23.760
while I was in law school.

2142
01:30:23.760 --> 01:30:25.090
So the Pan-Am games

2143
01:30:25.090 --> 01:30:27.340
was that while you were in law school?

2144
01:30:27.340 --> 01:30:28.813
It was that's?

2145
01:30:28.813 --> 01:30:31.260
That's crazy balancing those two things at that time.

2146
01:30:31.260 --> 01:30:33.240
Well, for me it was almost a sense of relief

2147
01:30:33.240 --> 01:30:36.083
because once I was in law school,

2148
01:30:36.940 --> 01:30:40.460
there was another outlet for energy.

2149
01:30:40.460 --> 01:30:42.930
There was another way of thinking. I mean,

2150
01:30:42.930 --> 01:30:44.270
when I was out in Michigan,

2151
01:30:44.270 --> 01:30:46.050
I was running, I was eating, I was sleeping

2152
01:30:46.050 --> 01:30:47.960
and that was pretty much it.

2153
01:30:47.960 --> 01:30:49.740
And to have another concentration

2154
01:30:49.740 --> 01:30:50.780
was actually kind of nice for me

2155
01:30:50.780 --> 01:30:55.780
because I wasn't entirely dependent on a successful race

2156
01:30:56.440 --> 01:30:58.720
to get a paycheck, you know?

2157
01:30:58.720 --> 01:31:01.150
And I think that in many respects was helpful

2158
01:31:01.150 --> 01:31:02.670
and freeing in a way that actually led

2159
01:31:02.670 --> 01:31:04.600
to some of my best running,

2160
01:31:04.600 --> 01:31:06.960
but it was also monotonous because, and lonely,

2161
01:31:06.960 --> 01:31:08.940
and I'm an extrovert I love people.

2162
01:31:08.940 --> 01:31:10.220
I love talking to people

2163
01:31:10.220 --> 01:31:11.860
I'm so honored to talk to,

2164
01:31:11.860 --> 01:31:14.670
the great philosopher Rich Roll right now,

2165
01:31:14.670 --> 01:31:17.263
who happens to be in our city, which is incredible,

2166
01:31:19.820 --> 01:31:22.730
but you spend hours and hours in the books

2167
01:31:22.730 --> 01:31:25.810
and studying for a law school exam.

2168
01:31:25.810 --> 01:31:29.727
And then you spend hours and hours out doing 15

2169
01:31:29.727 --> 01:31:33.464
and seven double days, which is boring.

2170
01:31:33.464 --> 01:31:35.420
Do you do that on your own or were you running

2171
01:31:35.420 --> 01:31:39.010
with a group like a crew when you were in law school?

2172
01:31:39.010 --> 01:31:41.090
The vast majority of it was on my own.

2173
01:31:41.090 --> 01:31:44.500
I had a slew of buddies that I'd get out for runs

2174
01:31:44.500 --> 01:31:46.170
with, there was a Kenyan team

2175
01:31:46.170 --> 01:31:47.470
that was training a bit there too,

2176
01:31:47.470 --> 01:31:50.210
that I would run occasionally with Villanova.

2177
01:31:50.210 --> 01:31:51.850
They had a great team well,

2178
01:31:51.850 --> 01:31:55.150
so occasionally I'd run with a guy or two from there.

2179
01:31:55.150 --> 01:31:58.450
But no, by and large, it was on my own.

2180
01:31:58.450 --> 01:32:02.300
And went William and Mary on a running scholarship.

2181
01:32:02.300 --> 01:32:04.840
I had I think it was a half scholarship

2182
01:32:04.840 --> 01:32:07.730
for most of the time, but yes.

2183
01:32:07.730 --> 01:32:12.270
Went to William and Mary and ran through college.

2184
01:32:12.270 --> 01:32:14.740
I was probably, I probably took it far too seriously,

2185
01:32:14.740 --> 01:32:18.960
almost at the time, but it was a wonderful spot as well.

2186
01:32:18.960 --> 01:32:20.620
And grew up in DC

2187
01:32:20.620 --> 01:32:21.700
Grew up in Northern Virginia.

2188
01:32:21.700 --> 01:32:23.290
I grew up in Bethesda.

2189
01:32:23.290 --> 01:32:25.588
There you go just to cross the Potomac High School.

2190
01:32:25.588 --> 01:32:26.421
What high school did you go to?

2191
01:32:26.421 --> 01:32:28.120
I went to Oakton High School.

2192
01:32:28.120 --> 01:32:30.890
Yeah, we had a hell of a team at the time.

2193
01:32:30.890 --> 01:32:32.150
Reston area?

2194
01:32:32.150 --> 01:32:33.500
Yeah, very nearby Reston,

2195
01:32:35.560 --> 01:32:38.680
actually in Reston Alan Webb who was just,

2196
01:32:38.680 --> 01:32:41.580
a year and a half or so younger than I was.

2197
01:32:41.580 --> 01:32:43.670
And so he was a training partner

2198
01:32:43.670 --> 01:32:45.840
through the most successful period

2199
01:32:45.840 --> 01:32:48.020
of my running career, which was around 2007.

2200
01:32:48.020 --> 01:32:50.860
And Alan was busy setting the American record

2201
01:32:50.860 --> 01:32:53.810
in the mile and he was the fastest person ever.

2202
01:32:53.810 --> 01:32:55.007
And I was training for the marathon

2203
01:32:55.007 --> 01:32:57.977
and which was my top year back in 2007 as well,

2204
01:32:57.977 --> 01:33:01.110
and so different events.

2205
01:33:01.110 --> 01:33:03.180
And he was obviously far more accomplished than I,

2206
01:33:03.180 --> 01:33:06.042
but a good buddy anyway.

2207
01:33:06.042 --> 01:33:08.420
Yeah, so you go to the, you make the Team USA,

2208
01:33:08.420 --> 01:33:11.210
Pan-American team 2007

2209
01:33:13.990 --> 01:33:15.460
that's where you run your PR right.

2210
01:33:15.460 --> 01:33:18.290
To two 16 and then you won the Austin Marathon,

2211
01:33:18.290 --> 01:33:19.880
like the year following.

2212
01:33:19.880 --> 01:33:22.430
Yup, Maybe six or seven months later.

2213
01:33:22.430 --> 01:33:24.873
I mean, the thing that I am most proud of,

2214
01:33:26.190 --> 01:33:28.920
in my running career is that,

2215
01:33:28.920 --> 01:33:30.130
well, let me take a step back.

2216
01:33:30.130 --> 01:33:34.890
My whole goal since I was like a ten-year-old

2217
01:33:34.890 --> 01:33:36.453
was to run for Team USA.

2218
01:33:37.540 --> 01:33:39.630
There is a tingle that you get

2219
01:33:39.630 --> 01:33:42.440
when you put on the USA Jersey

2220
01:33:42.440 --> 01:33:45.450
that you just don't get when you're aware and Saucony

2221
01:33:45.450 --> 01:33:47.880
or Brooks or some other brand.

2222
01:33:47.880 --> 01:33:52.400
And my goal was not just to represent Team USA,

2223
01:33:52.400 --> 01:33:54.380
but also run my absolute best.

2224
01:33:54.380 --> 01:33:57.510
And so I represented Team USA twice at the Pan Am Games

2225
01:33:57.510 --> 01:33:59.710
where I set my personal record.

2226
01:33:59.710 --> 01:34:04.330
And then at the Austin Marathon that I won.

2227
01:34:04.330 --> 01:34:07.040
And after that, I was, that was it,

2228
01:34:07.040 --> 01:34:09.100
I kinda got, I got done what I wanted to do.

2229
01:34:09.100 --> 01:34:12.730
And I had the USA Jersey, which I now brag about constantly

2230
01:34:12.730 --> 01:34:14.623
and I hang in my office at city hall.

2231
01:34:15.620 --> 01:34:18.380
And now that life is certainly a past history,

2232
01:34:18.380 --> 01:34:21.390
but I love talking about it still.

2233
01:34:21.390 --> 01:34:22.223
Yeah, of course.

2234
01:34:22.223 --> 01:34:24.220
Well running, but running still may,

2235
01:34:24.220 --> 01:34:26.610
maintains a huge place in your life.

2236
01:34:26.610 --> 01:34:28.870
It is, there's,
You're pretty trim.

2237
01:34:28.870 --> 01:34:31.070
So you're getting out there at least.

2238
01:34:31.070 --> 01:34:33.286
I think I'd be trimmed no matter what,

2239
01:34:33.286 --> 01:34:36.410
it's just kind of the physique or my biology,

2240
01:34:36.410 --> 01:34:38.600
but I mean, there's something so simplistic

2241
01:34:38.600 --> 01:34:42.360
and beautiful about running that I still really love.

2242
01:34:42.360 --> 01:34:44.510
And it's this direct correlation

2243
01:34:44.510 --> 01:34:46.410
between hard work and success.

2244
01:34:46.410 --> 01:34:49.230
That's why I originally fell in love with the sport.

2245
01:34:49.230 --> 01:34:51.100
If you work harder than the guy

2246
01:34:51.100 --> 01:34:52.910
that's standing next to you on the starting line,

2247
01:34:52.910 --> 01:34:54.480
you're probably gonna win.

2248
01:34:54.480 --> 01:34:55.370
And that's also kind of

2249
01:34:55.370 --> 01:34:57.460
what's incentivized me to get into politics,

2250
01:34:57.460 --> 01:35:02.363
was there is a lack of that direct correlation in society.

2251
01:35:03.849 --> 01:35:06.380
If I work hard,

2252
01:35:06.380 --> 01:35:09.010
there perhaps might be more of a direct correlation

2253
01:35:09.010 --> 01:35:10.210
with success in somebody else,

2254
01:35:10.210 --> 01:35:11.860
depending on where they grew up

2255
01:35:11.860 --> 01:35:12.920
or who their parents were,

2256
01:35:12.920 --> 01:35:15.120
or the socioeconomic background race,

2257
01:35:15.120 --> 01:35:18.800
all those things obviously contribute to the likelihood

2258
01:35:19.780 --> 01:35:21.810
of whether you see that direct correlation or not.

2259
01:35:21.810 --> 01:35:23.020
And that's part of it.

2260
01:35:23.020 --> 01:35:24.690
Yeah, running is, I mean,

2261
01:35:24.690 --> 01:35:26.240
certainly there's plenty of variables

2262
01:35:26.240 --> 01:35:29.880
that go into success, but it's relatively binary

2263
01:35:29.880 --> 01:35:31.940
in terms of that with the work in

2264
01:35:31.940 --> 01:35:34.273
kind of result out aspect of it.

2265
01:35:35.600 --> 01:35:37.560
And I'm interested in exploring like

2266
01:35:37.560 --> 01:35:39.740
how that spills into

2267
01:35:39.740 --> 01:35:44.560
how you think about policymaking and leading a city,

2268
01:35:44.560 --> 01:35:48.060
but at the same time, you're contending with,

2269
01:35:48.060 --> 01:35:49.700
a multitude of variables,

2270
01:35:49.700 --> 01:35:51.640
most of which you have no control over.

2271
01:35:51.640 --> 01:35:53.960
So the kind of work in result out thing

2272
01:35:53.960 --> 01:35:57.130
starts to break down a little bit in terms of,

2273
01:35:57.130 --> 01:36:00.323
how you kind of make hay with your job.

2274
01:36:01.320 --> 01:36:03.200
It does, but there are also external factors

2275
01:36:03.200 --> 01:36:04.700
associated with running as well.

2276
01:36:04.700 --> 01:36:06.913
I mean, people get busted up and hurt.

2277
01:36:07.750 --> 01:36:11.490
You know, performance is not necessarily dependent entirely

2278
01:36:11.490 --> 01:36:13.210
on your desire to succeed,

2279
01:36:13.210 --> 01:36:15.720
but your physical condition at the time,

2280
01:36:15.720 --> 01:36:17.470
sometimes you just have an off day,

2281
01:36:18.380 --> 01:36:23.380
but I mean, what I continue to hold pride in,

2282
01:36:23.840 --> 01:36:25.970
I often look back on is,

2283
01:36:25.970 --> 01:36:29.910
those moments when things were clicking beautifully

2284
01:36:29.910 --> 01:36:31.940
and running, and I mean,

2285
01:36:31.940 --> 01:36:33.870
those are some of the best days of my entire life.

2286
01:36:33.870 --> 01:36:38.440
I am so lucky in that the best run that I had,

2287
01:36:38.440 --> 01:36:40.210
race maintenance run, workout,

2288
01:36:40.210 --> 01:36:43.320
the best run in my entire life happened

2289
01:36:43.320 --> 01:36:47.550
to be my most important one, which was at the Pan-Am Games.

2290
01:36:47.550 --> 01:36:51.550
I mean, it was one of those days where I was wearing USA.

2291
01:36:51.550 --> 01:36:53.510
And so I was on, I was on top of the world.

2292
01:36:53.510 --> 01:36:58.510
I felt so good and I remember being about halfway

2293
01:37:00.880 --> 01:37:04.870
into the race and I was way back,

2294
01:37:04.870 --> 01:37:07.240
but where I was certainly predicted to be,

2295
01:37:07.240 --> 01:37:09.070
I was one of perhaps the last individuals

2296
01:37:09.070 --> 01:37:10.510
that get the entrance

2297
01:37:10.510 --> 01:37:13.610
and then you start to pass one after another, after another.

2298
01:37:13.610 --> 01:37:17.290
And then, the oxygen just starts falling into your lungs

2299
01:37:17.290 --> 01:37:20.110
your muscles are itching to run faster.

2300
01:37:20.110 --> 01:37:22.490
And I remember thinking to myself,

2301
01:37:22.490 --> 01:37:24.550
like as couldn't get any better,

2302
01:37:24.550 --> 01:37:26.463
I've never felt so good in my entire life.

2303
01:37:26.463 --> 01:37:28.223
And then it felt then it started raining,

2304
01:37:29.080 --> 01:37:31.330
which was a huge advantage for me.

2305
01:37:31.330 --> 01:37:33.810
I thought a one, cause I love running in the rain,

2306
01:37:33.810 --> 01:37:37.010
but two, I sweat like none other when I'm running

2307
01:37:37.010 --> 01:37:40.260
and so my shoes get really heavy.

2308
01:37:40.260 --> 01:37:42.510
So I figured this was the great evenor

2309
01:37:42.510 --> 01:37:43.950
because everybody else's shoes would be heavy too

2310
01:37:43.950 --> 01:37:47.450
and I thought this is just, everything was coming.

2311
01:37:47.450 --> 01:37:50.060
Those rare moments where everything just kind

2312
01:37:50.060 --> 01:37:52.373
of comes together in a perfect storm.

2313
01:37:53.310 --> 01:37:55.350
So yeah, you did the thing.

2314
01:37:55.350 --> 01:37:57.730
Did the thing called it a day,

2315
01:37:57.730 --> 01:37:59.000
moved on to the next thing.

2316
01:37:59.000 --> 01:37:59.890
Yeah, what's interesting

2317
01:37:59.890 --> 01:38:04.200
I think even more broadly about the relationship

2318
01:38:04.200 --> 01:38:08.460
between kind of running and what you do now

2319
01:38:08.460 --> 01:38:11.770
is that fundamentally you're an endurance athlete

2320
01:38:11.770 --> 01:38:16.270
and Minneapolis is in an endurance event right now.

2321
01:38:16.270 --> 01:38:18.130
And what you've learned as an endurance athlete

2322
01:38:18.130 --> 01:38:22.350
is it's about like the consistency, the persistence,

2323
