that are happening right now

2580
01:50:42.470 --> 01:50:45.920
in that you have these dualities of both

2581
01:50:45.920 --> 01:50:48.273
of which are true, you know,

2582
01:50:49.570 --> 01:50:52.330
wonderful place to racial justice and reckoning

2583
01:50:52.330 --> 01:50:55.080
and instances when it's not safe due

2584
01:50:55.080 --> 01:50:57.343
to other outside factors.

2585
01:50:58.690 --> 01:51:02.710
Policing, we need deep structural change

2586
01:51:02.710 --> 01:51:05.130
to how our police department operates.

2587
01:51:05.130 --> 01:51:07.570
We need safety beyond policing

2588
01:51:07.570 --> 01:51:11.610
because not every 911 call needs a response

2589
01:51:11.610 --> 01:51:15.903
from an officer with a gun and we need police.

2590
01:51:17.100 --> 01:51:19.200
There are horrible situations

2591
01:51:19.200 --> 01:51:23.750
that we make officers respond to that nobody else wants

2592
01:51:23.750 --> 01:51:24.893
to nor can they.

2593
01:51:25.930 --> 01:51:27.860
And both of those things are true.

2594
01:51:27.860 --> 01:51:30.720
I think the police reform conversation

2595
01:51:30.720 --> 01:51:32.670
is the best kind of example,

2596
01:51:32.670 --> 01:51:35.300
to explore how truth lives in nuance,

2597
01:51:35.300 --> 01:51:38.510
because it's very easy to lapse into this notion

2598
01:51:38.510 --> 01:51:40.640
that with respect to Minneapolis,

2599
01:51:40.640 --> 01:51:44.060
at least it's either defund the police,

2600
01:51:44.060 --> 01:51:45.360
which means a million different things

2601
01:51:45.360 --> 01:51:46.920
to a million different people,

2602
01:51:46.920 --> 01:51:51.220
but is often characterized as complete abolition

2603
01:51:51.220 --> 01:51:55.490
of the police or you're pro military militarization

2604
01:51:55.490 --> 01:51:56.600
of the police.

2605
01:51:56.600 --> 01:51:58.338
It's an either or situation

2606
01:51:58.338 --> 01:52:00.090
and the longer that I'm here

2607
01:52:00.090 --> 01:52:02.860
and the more people that I talk to and the deeper,

2608
01:52:02.860 --> 01:52:06.430
I kind of educate myself about this issue.

2609
01:52:06.430 --> 01:52:08.730
It doesn't seem like there's that much difference

2610
01:52:08.730 --> 01:52:12.730
between most of the voices who have a say in policymaking.

2611
01:52:12.730 --> 01:52:14.400
It seems that everyone agrees

2612
01:52:14.400 --> 01:52:17.430
that the police need deep reform.

2613
01:52:17.430 --> 01:52:18.740
Now there's quibbling over

2614
01:52:18.740 --> 01:52:19.930
what that might look like

2615
01:52:19.930 --> 01:52:21.993
or how that gets implemented.

2616
01:52:22.830 --> 01:52:27.830
But aside from an extreme, extreme fringe,

2617
01:52:28.570 --> 01:52:30.470
there's nobody who's really saying,

2618
01:52:30.470 --> 01:52:33.830
we need to abolish the police department completely.

2619
01:52:33.830 --> 01:52:34.960
At least as far as I'm aware,

2620
01:52:34.960 --> 01:52:36.480
I don't know You're shaking your head

2621
01:52:36.480 --> 01:52:39.220
maybe you can tell me more about that.

2622
01:52:39.220 --> 01:52:41.700
And it's really a conversation about

2623
01:52:41.700 --> 01:52:44.860
how to best accomplish this goal,

2624
01:52:44.860 --> 01:52:49.810
that there seems to be somewhat of a consensus on

2625
01:52:49.810 --> 01:52:51.377
is that wrong or what do you think?

2626
01:52:51.377 --> 01:52:53.820
For the most part you're right,

2627
01:52:53.820 --> 01:52:58.010
following some of those most tumultuous times

2628
01:52:58.010 --> 01:52:59.863
after George Floyd was killed,

2629
01:53:00.810 --> 01:53:02.060
there were definitely people

2630
01:53:02.060 --> 01:53:05.150
that were calling for getting rid of the police.

2631
01:53:05.150 --> 01:53:09.610
Sure, sure can we talk about the video?

2632
01:53:09.610 --> 01:53:10.712
Sorry about the video.

2633
01:53:10.712 --> 01:53:12.999
(laughs)

2634
01:53:12.999 --> 01:53:14.937
I'm never gonna live that one down.

2635
01:53:14.937 --> 01:53:15.770
I mean, a lot of people probably

2636
01:53:15.770 --> 01:53:16.850
who are listening probably saw it,

2637
01:53:16.850 --> 01:53:18.970
but correct me if I'm wrong.

2638
01:53:18.970 --> 01:53:20.580
It was in the midst of a protest

2639
01:53:20.580 --> 01:53:22.490
you were kind of ushered out of your house

2640
01:53:22.490 --> 01:53:25.830
and you joined this procession into Downtown

2641
01:53:25.830 --> 01:53:28.533
and in front of a large number of people,

2642
01:53:30.300 --> 01:53:35.120
a woman, I forget her name sort of publicly asked you

2643
01:53:35.120 --> 01:53:37.100
point blank and yes or no fashion,

2644
01:53:37.100 --> 01:53:39.810
if you were in favor of defunding the police

2645
01:53:39.810 --> 01:53:44.810
and you said that if she meant abolishing the police,

2646
01:53:44.990 --> 01:53:46.590
that no, you did not support that.

2647
01:53:46.590 --> 01:53:50.400
And that resulted in you being essentially kind

2648
01:53:50.400 --> 01:53:53.156
of told to leave and shame on you

2649
01:53:53.156 --> 01:53:54.670
and all that kind of stuff.

2650
01:53:54.670 --> 01:53:56.480
And there's a video of you kind of walking

2651
01:53:56.480 --> 01:53:58.480
through the procession of people

2652
01:53:58.480 --> 01:54:00.790
that a lot of people saw and that kind

2653
01:54:00.790 --> 01:54:04.120
of became the lightning rod moment

2654
01:54:04.120 --> 01:54:06.320
in terms of how people were kind

2655
01:54:06.320 --> 01:54:09.410
of contextualizing this debate.

2656
01:54:09.410 --> 01:54:10.243
Yeah, it was tough.

2657
01:54:10.243 --> 01:54:13.393
And let me tell you, I'm proud of that moment.

2658
01:54:16.022 --> 01:54:18.120
I'm proud because I told the truth

2659
01:54:18.120 --> 01:54:20.113
when it was especially hard,

2660
01:54:22.261 --> 01:54:23.750
it was a large group, as you mentioned

2661
01:54:23.750 --> 01:54:26.193
of those protesting that came to my home.

2662
01:54:27.080 --> 01:54:29.820
That demanded that I come out

2663
01:54:29.820 --> 01:54:32.375
that were asking for answers.

2664
01:54:32.375 --> 01:54:33.890
I came out of my home

2665
01:54:33.890 --> 01:54:37.973
and I was asked to come up nearby the stage area.

2666
01:54:38.870 --> 01:54:42.830
And yeah, I was asked if I would be willing

2667
01:54:42.830 --> 01:54:46.083
to commit there on the spot to defunding the police.

2668
01:54:47.580 --> 01:54:49.210
And I said very clearly

2669
01:54:49.210 --> 01:54:51.530
that I am for deep structural change

2670
01:54:51.530 --> 01:54:55.450
of a system that in so many cases has been racist.

2671
01:54:55.450 --> 01:54:57.960
I think we need safety beyond policing,

2672
01:54:57.960 --> 01:54:58.793
but let me ask you,

2673
01:54:58.793 --> 01:55:00.550
what do you mean by that defund the police.

2674
01:55:00.550 --> 01:55:01.910
And she said very clearly,

2675
01:55:01.910 --> 01:55:03.410
I mean, get rid of the police.

2676
01:55:04.610 --> 01:55:08.440
And my answer is no, because that's the truth.

2677
01:55:13.060 --> 01:55:15.170
Now the next day I'll note

2678
01:55:15.170 --> 01:55:16.970
that there was a strong majority.

2679
01:55:16.970 --> 01:55:19.190
In fact, the veto proof majority of the city council

2680
01:55:19.190 --> 01:55:21.010
that came back and took the same pledge

2681
01:55:21.010 --> 01:55:25.690
that I refused to take and have since almost

2682
01:55:25.690 --> 01:55:27.993
every one of them gone back on it.

2683
01:55:29.270 --> 01:55:31.530
I'm not gonna say that I'm gonna do something

2684
01:55:31.530 --> 01:55:33.593
that I'm not going to follow through on.

2685
01:55:34.840 --> 01:55:37.113
And yeah, it was tough.

2686
01:55:38.290 --> 01:55:41.350
I mean, it doesn't tickle to get

2687
01:55:41.350 --> 01:55:45.753
Game of Thrones style shamed in front of the national media.

2688
01:55:46.970 --> 01:55:51.970
But when I talk to black and brown communities in our city,

2689
01:55:52.580 --> 01:55:54.300
when I visit the north side,

2690
01:55:54.300 --> 01:55:56.193
which I do all the time,

2691
01:55:58.250 --> 01:56:00.850
there's a couple of comments that I get

2692
01:56:00.850 --> 01:56:05.450
the first is thank you for immediately terminating

2693
01:56:05.450 --> 01:56:08.040
those officers and calling for charges.

2694
01:56:08.040 --> 01:56:10.080
I mean, there was unanimous praise for you,

2695
01:56:10.080 --> 01:56:12.660
getting on that, like immediately,

2696
01:56:12.660 --> 01:56:14.760
because that has not been handled

2697
01:56:14.760 --> 01:56:18.653
in that type of fashion in other instances, in other cities.

2698
01:56:20.300 --> 01:56:22.270
But the second thing that I hear

2699
01:56:22.270 --> 01:56:24.320
from our black and brown communities

2700
01:56:24.320 --> 01:56:26.370
is thank you for standing with our chief

2701
01:56:26.370 --> 01:56:30.033
and not agreeing to defund and abolish the police.

2702
01:56:31.240 --> 01:56:32.262
And again...

2703
01:56:32.262 --> 01:56:35.150
So what do you make of that?

2704
01:56:35.150 --> 01:56:38.020
Well, certainly no community is a monolith,

2705
01:56:38.020 --> 01:56:41.620
but if it's based on what I hear,

2706
01:56:41.620 --> 01:56:42.780
when I'm out on the street,

2707
01:56:42.780 --> 01:56:45.363
if it's based on what the polling clearly says,

2708
01:56:46.680 --> 01:56:50.100
it's dead in line our, again,

2709
01:56:50.100 --> 01:56:51.790
no community is a monolith.

2710
01:56:51.790 --> 01:56:53.320
But what we hear loud and clear

2711
01:56:53.320 --> 01:56:56.440
is that they want deep structural change.

2712
01:56:56.440 --> 01:56:59.653
Do they want the funding, no.

2713
01:57:00.610 --> 01:57:04.640
And there has been some pretty significant reformations

2714
01:57:04.640 --> 01:57:06.060
in terms of the police, right?

2715
01:57:06.060 --> 01:57:09.430
With the body cams and the ending

2716
01:57:09.430 --> 01:57:11.570
of the kind of warrior training

2717
01:57:11.570 --> 01:57:13.158
and a couple other things along the way.

2718
01:57:13.158 --> 01:57:14.023
The homework I'm impressed,

2719
01:57:14.910 --> 01:57:17.783
Yeah, well we've made some major changes.

2720
01:57:19.490 --> 01:57:23.530
We added a disciplinary matrix to our body camera policy.

2721
01:57:23.530 --> 01:57:26.920
When I came into office was about 55% compliance

2722
01:57:26.920 --> 01:57:29.100
with our body camera policy.

2723
01:57:29.100 --> 01:57:31.180
Now there's 95% compliance.

2724
01:57:31.180 --> 01:57:33.430
So in other words, before 55% of the time

2725
01:57:33.430 --> 01:57:35.228
they were turning on their body cameras.

2726
01:57:35.228 --> 01:57:36.610
And there's like a consequence.

2727
01:57:36.610 --> 01:57:38.620
If like, if that's not true.

2728
01:57:38.620 --> 01:57:40.710
They don't turn them on when they should,

2729
01:57:40.710 --> 01:57:43.763
there are consequences to it.

2730
01:57:44.865 --> 01:57:45.865
The other penalties,

2731
01:57:46.820 --> 01:57:48.840
we were the first city in the entire country

2732
01:57:48.840 --> 01:57:52.580
to ban warrior style training, both on and off duty.

2733
01:57:52.580 --> 01:57:54.850
We overhauled our use of force policy,

2734
01:57:54.850 --> 01:57:57.210
we banned No-knock warrants for all,

2735
01:57:57.210 --> 01:57:59.700
but exigent circumstances,

2736
01:57:59.700 --> 01:58:04.350
we've ensured that deescalation would be added

2737
01:58:04.350 --> 01:58:07.520
and embedded in everything that our officer is doing

2738
01:58:07.520 --> 01:58:10.300
by requirement and by the reporting structure,

2739
01:58:10.300 --> 01:58:11.400
how it's set up.

2740
01:58:11.400 --> 01:58:12.523
I mean, I can go on,

2741
01:58:12.523 --> 01:58:14.450
there's a litany of other changes that we've made,

2742
01:58:14.450 --> 01:58:15.400
but here's the truth,

2743
01:58:15.400 --> 01:58:18.600
is that gonna change the culture of our police department

2744
01:58:18.600 --> 01:58:19.660
or any police department?

2745
01:58:19.660 --> 01:58:22.600
No, it won't, these are policies changes.

2746
01:58:22.600 --> 01:58:26.550
So to what extent do they fundamentally alter anything

2747
01:58:26.550 --> 01:58:28.720
or are they just band-aids on a system

2748
01:58:28.720 --> 01:58:31.770
that needs a deeper kind of redress?

2749
01:58:31.770 --> 01:58:34.463
I think they're important changes that will help,

2750
01:58:37.120 --> 01:58:40.190
but when you talk about deeper redress,

2751
01:58:40.190 --> 01:58:42.933
let's be specific about what we're talking about.

2752
01:58:45.330 --> 01:58:47.410
One of the things that I think would make a huge difference

2753
01:58:47.410 --> 01:58:48.753
is arbitration reform.

2754
01:58:49.898 --> 01:58:51.280
And then I'll get a little specific on this

2755
01:58:51.280 --> 01:58:53.030
so we might end up just cutting this part out,

2756
01:58:53.030 --> 01:58:54.660
but this is a big deal.

2757
01:58:54.660 --> 01:58:59.660
And I think every mayor, every chief is hindered by this,

2758
01:59:01.130 --> 01:59:03.183
these laws throughout the whole country.

2759
01:59:04.450 --> 01:59:07.263
When chief are down to, or I fire a police officer,

2760
01:59:08.110 --> 01:59:11.570
it is mandated that they go through this arbitration system

2761
01:59:11.570 --> 01:59:15.040
that sees 50% of those termination

2762
01:59:15.040 --> 01:59:18.840
and disciplinary decisions come right back to the city.

2763
01:59:18.840 --> 01:59:21.660
So 50% of the time when we fire somebody,

2764
01:59:21.660 --> 01:59:23.030
that decision is not upheld

2765
01:59:23.030 --> 01:59:25.280
because of this mandatory arbitration

2766
01:59:25.280 --> 01:59:26.433
that needs to change.

2767
01:59:27.550 --> 01:59:30.250
I think especially with an instances of egregious

2768
01:59:30.250 --> 01:59:34.610
use of force or lying on a formal document

2769
01:59:34.610 --> 01:59:35.980
that should change.

2770
01:59:35.980 --> 01:59:39.010
And there's also a deeper issue as well,

2771
01:59:39.010 --> 01:59:41.883
even beyond that, which is,

2772
01:59:43.230 --> 01:59:46.580
if you are an organization that is known

2773
01:59:46.580 --> 01:59:48.360
for being something,

2774
01:59:48.360 --> 01:59:53.360
if you're a podcast that is known for attracting cool,

2775
01:59:54.790 --> 01:59:58.633
health oriented people with a good suntan,

2776
02:00:01.418 --> 02:00:03.827
and then you go out and recruit

2777
02:00:04.710 --> 02:00:06.920
to get additional people to watch your podcast,

2778
02:00:06.920 --> 02:00:07.790
who are you gonna attract?

2779
02:00:07.790 --> 02:00:09.790
You can do that kind of similarly

2780
02:00:09.790 --> 02:00:12.863
if you're a police department that is known for something,

2781
02:00:13.730 --> 02:00:16.560
whether that's for being compassionate and service oriented

2782
02:00:16.560 --> 02:00:19.030
or that's for being a bunch of racists

2783
02:00:19.030 --> 02:00:20.180
and you go out and recruit,

2784
02:00:20.180 --> 02:00:21.620
who do you think you're gonna get?

2785
02:00:21.620 --> 02:00:25.130
And so it makes it all the more important

2786
02:00:25.130 --> 02:00:27.330
that you do this necessary work ahead

2787
02:00:27.330 --> 02:00:29.017
of time to change both the reality

2788
02:00:29.017 --> 02:00:31.100
and the perception so that you can get

2789
02:00:31.100 --> 02:00:32.750
the right individuals in

2790
02:00:32.750 --> 02:00:34.543
and then the wrong individuals out.

2791
02:00:35.570 --> 02:00:37.390
When you're faced with having

2792
02:00:37.390 --> 02:00:40.820
to make an important decision,

2793
02:00:40.820 --> 02:00:42.700
which you're doing constantly.

2794
02:00:42.700 --> 02:00:45.290
And you've got all these different voices,

2795
02:00:45.290 --> 02:00:47.770
and everybody's got a perspective and a point of view,

2796
02:00:47.770 --> 02:00:50.310
and these people are donors or their voters,

2797
02:00:50.310 --> 02:00:51.993
or they're coming with,

2798
02:00:52.900 --> 02:00:55.940
what they're telling you comes with a block of voters

2799
02:00:55.940 --> 02:00:58.490
and they can move that one way or the other.

2800
02:00:58.490 --> 02:01:01.420
What is your process for getting clarity

2801
02:01:01.420 --> 02:01:03.730
so that you can, tell the truth

2802
02:01:03.730 --> 02:01:07.190
or vote with your conscious or make the best decision?

2803
02:01:07.190 --> 02:01:09.660
Like, is there a method to how you do that,

2804
02:01:09.660 --> 02:01:11.410
under extreme duress.

2805
02:01:11.410 --> 02:01:13.350
Under times of extreme duress,

2806
02:01:13.350 --> 02:01:18.350
especially when you're going through a major crisis.

2807
02:01:18.920 --> 02:01:21.010
The truth is that you can't,

2808
02:01:21.010 --> 02:01:24.070
send out a poll to determine how every last individual

2809
02:01:24.070 --> 02:01:24.913
is feeling.

2810
02:01:25.830 --> 02:01:27.260
You have community partners,

2811
02:01:27.260 --> 02:01:31.220
you have mentors and individuals within the city

2812
02:01:31.220 --> 02:01:34.253
that you look to, that you talk to the get input from.

2813
02:01:35.320 --> 02:01:38.740
Usually I try and take multiple sides in,

2814
02:01:38.740 --> 02:01:42.010
hear different arguments in play devil's advocate,

2815
02:01:42.010 --> 02:01:43.113
wherever I can.

2816
02:01:44.102 --> 02:01:45.593
And then you gotta make a decision.

2817
02:01:46.830 --> 02:01:50.850
And hopefully most of the time you get that decision right

2818
02:01:50.850 --> 02:01:51.863
sometimes you don't.

2819
02:01:52.980 --> 02:01:55.070
And that's the nature of being mayor,

2820
02:01:55.070 --> 02:01:57.160
I mean, it's different by the way

2821
02:01:57.160 --> 02:01:59.410
than when you're in like Congress,

2822
02:01:59.410 --> 02:02:01.493
I don't vote, I don't have a vote.

2823
02:02:02.570 --> 02:02:03.960
I'm tasked with making decisions

2824
02:02:03.960 --> 02:02:06.540
in crisis oriented situations.

2825
02:02:06.540 --> 02:02:08.947
And I mean, there's an old LBJ quote that says,

2826
02:02:08.947 --> 02:02:11.557
"when the burdens of the presidency get too heavy to carry,

2827
02:02:11.557 --> 02:02:13.537
"I just, thank God I'm not a mayor."

2828
02:02:14.710 --> 02:02:17.400
We are dealing with all of the other situations

2829
02:02:17.400 --> 02:02:19.693
that nobody wants to handle mayors are.

2830
02:02:20.640 --> 02:02:22.060
I I've half joked that the,

2831
02:02:22.060 --> 02:02:24.070
one of the main jobs of being mayor,

2832
02:02:24.070 --> 02:02:25.650
especially right now in Minneapolis,

2833
02:02:25.650 --> 02:02:27.740
in other cities in the country,

2834
02:02:27.740 --> 02:02:32.740
is to jump on all of the grenades of societal shortcomings.

2835
02:02:33.230 --> 02:02:35.310
And you still get blamed by anybody

2836
02:02:35.310 --> 02:02:37.316
that gets hit by the truck.

2837
02:02:37.316 --> 02:02:39.516
I mean, this is like the era of the mayor,

2838
02:02:40.445 --> 02:02:41.860
like when have we been talking about city mayors,

2839
02:02:41.860 --> 02:02:43.560
as much as we've been talking about them

2840
02:02:43.560 --> 02:02:45.990
over the last 18 months, it's crazy.

2841
02:02:45.990 --> 02:02:47.530
And it's a unique and weird job

2842
02:02:47.530 --> 02:02:50.810
because on some level it really is about grassroots,

2843
02:02:50.810 --> 02:02:54.720
local politics, but mayors do wield a lot of power

2844
02:02:54.720 --> 02:02:59.060
to make swift rapid and significant changes.

2845
02:02:59.060 --> 02:03:01.910
And that obviously provokes the ire of a lot of people

2846
02:03:01.910 --> 02:03:03.940
when it doesn't swing in their direction.

2847
02:03:03.940 --> 02:03:07.550
Yeah, they both do and they don't at the same time.

2848
02:03:07.550 --> 02:03:12.550
I think the truth is that the things that intrigued you

2849
02:03:17.210 --> 02:03:20.240
and excite you, when you get passionate about,

2850
02:03:20.240 --> 02:03:25.240
on a very deep level, that's Congress,

2851
02:03:26.210 --> 02:03:28.120
that's the federal government in many cases,

2852
02:03:28.120 --> 02:03:33.090
that's healthcare, universal health care

2853
02:03:33.090 --> 02:03:38.090
and these broad sweeping, rights,

2854
02:03:38.810 --> 02:03:39.980
marriage equality, things like that.

2855
02:03:39.980 --> 02:03:42.320
There's a debate debated ad nauseum

2856
02:03:42.320 --> 02:03:44.660
and move at a very glacial pace.

2857
02:03:44.660 --> 02:03:47.200
Exactly and you can take a vote

2858
02:03:47.200 --> 02:03:48.510
and maybe there's a chance that

2859
02:03:48.510 --> 02:03:50.560
in a year or two, you see the impact from it,

2860
02:03:50.560 --> 02:03:53.250
the things that really piss you off,

2861
02:03:53.250 --> 02:03:55.230
usually that's what mayors are dealing with

2862
02:03:55.230 --> 02:03:57.160
now in these last couple of years,

2863
02:03:57.160 --> 02:03:59.290
mayors have been taking on more.

2864
02:03:59.290 --> 02:04:00.670
And I think rightfully so,

2865
02:04:00.670 --> 02:04:02.770
because there has been all of this gridlock

2866
02:04:02.770 --> 02:04:04.250
at state and federal legislatures.

2867
02:04:04.250 --> 02:04:06.060
And so mayors have just stepped in and said,

2868
02:04:06.060 --> 02:04:07.930
we're gonna just do it.

2869
02:04:07.930 --> 02:04:10.290
And we're gonna take the wrath

2870
02:04:10.290 --> 02:04:12.603
or the credit well, along with it.

2871
02:04:14.250 --> 02:04:16.710
But depending on which city you're in,

2872
02:04:16.710 --> 02:04:20.120
in many cases, mayors have all of the responsibility,

2873
02:04:20.120 --> 02:04:23.700
but none of the authority and in Minneapolis,

2874
02:04:23.700 --> 02:04:26.900
we have this kind of gray system

2875
02:04:26.900 --> 02:04:28.390
that is not a strong mayor system.

2876
02:04:28.390 --> 02:04:32.113
We do not have the system that Chicago has or St. Paul has.

2877
02:04:33.300 --> 02:04:36.360
We have a system that is more decentralized than that.

2878
02:04:36.360 --> 02:04:40.700
And so you oftentimes get blamed for things

2879
02:04:40.700 --> 02:04:41.533
that aren't deserved.

2880
02:04:41.533 --> 02:04:44.710
You also get credit for things that aren't deserved also.

2881
02:04:44.710 --> 02:04:47.020
And that the truth is always a little bit more greater

2882
02:04:47.020 --> 02:04:48.410
than it's presented.

2883
02:04:48.410 --> 02:04:50.140
What do you think,

2884
02:04:50.140 --> 02:04:52.060
like people fundamentally misunderstand

2885
02:04:52.060 --> 02:04:54.770
the most about what your job entails?

2886
02:04:54.770 --> 02:04:57.020
I mean, I think there's often the perception

2887
02:04:57.020 --> 02:04:58.590
that you can wave a magic wand

2888
02:04:58.590 --> 02:05:00.490
and make something happen immediately.

2889
02:05:02.090 --> 02:05:03.280
I think there's the perception

2890
02:05:03.280 --> 02:05:04.760
that you have authority over things

2891
02:05:04.760 --> 02:05:06.893
that you just don't have authority over.

2892
02:05:10.760 --> 02:05:12.970
And there's also all of the other aspects

2893
02:05:12.970 --> 02:05:15.913
that people don't see that happen behind the scenes.

2894
02:05:18.460 --> 02:05:20.270
There's these heartfelt conversations

2895
02:05:20.270 --> 02:05:21.793
with community members,

2896
02:05:23.710 --> 02:05:28.490
there are complexities that go well beyond a hashtag

2897
02:05:28.490 --> 02:05:31.010
on Twitter or a soundbite

2898
02:05:31.010 --> 02:05:33.683
that you'll get in a two minute news conference.

2899
02:05:34.580 --> 02:05:38.020
And often times people don't see that

2900
02:05:38.020 --> 02:05:40.133
and this is, I think at least in part,

2901
02:05:42.830 --> 02:05:46.110
a causal element of something that social media

2902
02:05:46.110 --> 02:05:48.100
has caused over times.

2903
02:05:48.100 --> 02:05:49.330
And there's certainly a lot of positives associated with it.

2904
02:05:49.330 --> 02:05:50.610
There's definitely negatives too.

2905
02:05:50.610 --> 02:05:55.610
Yeah, I mean, as somebody who lives in the media,

2906
02:05:56.270 --> 02:05:59.490
you're in the media constantly,

2907
02:05:59.490 --> 02:06:02.750
I don't know that you've done any podcasts.

2908
02:06:02.750 --> 02:06:04.730
Maybe you've done a podcast or two?

2909
02:06:04.730 --> 02:06:05.940
I did the daily.

2910
02:06:05.940 --> 02:06:07.961
You did. Oh, you did the daily.

2911
02:06:07.961 --> 02:06:09.760
But that's still like pretty, that's brief.

2912
02:06:09.760 --> 02:06:14.430
Like I wanted to give you the opportunity in kind of

2913
02:06:14.430 --> 02:06:16.180
a space where we can breathe,

2914
02:06:16.180 --> 02:06:17.660
where it's like safe,

2915
02:06:17.660 --> 02:06:22.430
where you can kind of express your perspectives

2916
02:06:22.430 --> 02:06:25.930
without fear of it getting chopped up and mis-characterized,

2917
02:06:25.930 --> 02:06:27.250
or taken out of context.

2918
02:06:27.250 --> 02:06:29.560
So in that therapeutic.

2919
02:06:29.560 --> 02:06:30.717
And so thanks that.

2920
02:06:30.717 --> 02:06:34.180
(overlapping conversation)

2921
02:06:34.180 --> 02:06:37.180
In that spirit, like I'm interested in,

2922
02:06:37.180 --> 02:06:39.110
it must be frustrating for you

2923
02:06:40.750 --> 02:06:44.200
when you see, you pop up in some news program

2924
02:06:44.200 --> 02:06:47.440
and they're taking some clip or you're,

2925
02:06:47.440 --> 02:06:49.910
participating in one of those squawk box things

2926
02:06:49.910 --> 02:06:51.790
where everyone's shouting and you have three seconds

2927
02:06:51.790 --> 02:06:53.920
to express your point of view.

2928
02:06:53.920 --> 02:06:58.620
What do you think in your message gets lost in that,

2929
02:06:58.620 --> 02:07:01.950
that you would want people to better understand?

2930
02:07:01.950 --> 02:07:03.650
I know it's a vague question, but.

2931
02:07:04.750 --> 02:07:05.810
There have been instances

2932
02:07:05.810 --> 02:07:08.360
when one particular event

2933
02:07:08.360 --> 02:07:12.913
or policy has been misconstrued for the most part,

2934
02:07:14.040 --> 02:07:15.370
I've actually really appreciated the way

2935
02:07:15.370 --> 02:07:17.393
that our media has handled things.

2936
02:07:19.050 --> 02:07:21.960
Of course they're issues that I'll always be off about.

2937
02:07:21.960 --> 02:07:23.033
That's inevitable.

2938
02:07:25.210 --> 02:07:28.300
What I see that is problematic though,

2939
02:07:28.300 --> 02:07:33.300
is the polar extremes.

2940
02:07:34.920 --> 02:07:38.320
You the very far white supremacis,

2941
02:07:39.250 --> 02:07:43.310
the very far and archist left spinning narratives

2942
02:07:43.310 --> 02:07:47.220
that just have no basis whatsoever in reality.

2943
02:07:47.220 --> 02:07:51.330
And also seem to push to dehumanize people

2944
02:07:51.330 --> 02:07:56.330
in a big way I am a human being,

2945
02:07:57.860 --> 02:08:02.720
I have skills, I have flaws, I'm a human being.

2946
02:08:02.720 --> 02:08:04.120
And every day you get up

2947
02:08:04.120 --> 02:08:06.780
and you try to do your very best,

2948
02:08:06.780 --> 02:08:10.000
you try to take an honest and compassionate approach.

2949
02:08:10.000 --> 02:08:15.000
And sometimes all that gets all that gets lost,

2950
02:08:16.440 --> 02:08:21.440
depending on how those that wanna speak up,

2951
02:08:22.630 --> 02:08:24.070
whether they like it or not.

2952
02:08:24.070 --> 02:08:25.300
Well, just by dent of being mayor,

2953
02:08:25.300 --> 02:08:28.470
you become the receivership for everybody's angst

2954
02:08:28.470 --> 02:08:30.220
and I, or whatever's bothering them,

2955
02:08:30.220 --> 02:08:31.790
or whatever problem that they're having.

2956
02:08:31.790 --> 02:08:33.700
Like, you're the target for them.

2957
02:08:33.700 --> 02:08:36.040
Well, and you see, I think you see both ends of it.

2958
02:08:36.040 --> 02:08:37.430
And I think this is probably the case

2959
02:08:37.430 --> 02:08:39.820
for so many probably elected officials

2960
02:08:39.820 --> 02:08:42.700
and I guess celebrities at times as well,

2961
02:08:42.700 --> 02:08:45.880
you're, neither is good nor are you as bad

2962
02:08:46.800 --> 02:08:51.103
as the media or social media makes you out to be,

2963
02:08:52.350 --> 02:08:57.350
there was a back in 2019,

2964
02:08:58.060 --> 02:09:00.493
I want to say there, Donald Trump came to town.

2965
02:09:03.240 --> 02:09:05.918
I know where you going just go ahead.

2966
02:09:05.918 --> 02:09:07.910
And Donald Trump came to town.

2967
02:09:07.910 --> 02:09:12.000
He, we were forced to basically foot the bill

2968
02:09:12.000 --> 02:09:15.480
for all of these major security expenses associated

2969
02:09:15.480 --> 02:09:17.463
with his visit to our city.

2970
02:09:18.400 --> 02:09:21.930
And I didn't wanna pay for those security expenses

2971
02:09:21.930 --> 02:09:23.500
I certainly, we want the taxpayer

2972
02:09:23.500 --> 02:09:25.530
to have to foot the bill.

2973
02:09:25.530 --> 02:09:28.730
And so we said directly to the campaign,

2974
02:09:28.730 --> 02:09:30.400
this was not even publicly at this point.

2975
02:09:30.400 --> 02:09:32.060
This was like, we just don't think it's right

2976
02:09:32.060 --> 02:09:33.486
for us to do this.

2977
02:09:33.486 --> 02:09:34.319
And then immediately,

2978
02:09:34.319 --> 02:09:35.890
the Republican party took it up that Donald Trump

2979
02:09:35.890 --> 02:09:37.550
started going after us on social media

2980
02:09:37.550 --> 02:09:38.550
and we responded it.

2981
02:09:40.415 --> 02:09:42.710
All it was, was a stupid tweet.

2982
02:09:42.710 --> 02:09:44.333
I didn't do anything heroic,

2983
02:09:45.230 --> 02:09:48.350
but you're suddenly made into Zack.

2984
02:09:48.350 --> 02:09:50.630
It's really put you in the national spotlight

2985
02:09:50.630 --> 02:09:53.460
in a big way, it was the idea was he was coming to town

2986
02:09:53.460 --> 02:09:55.070
for a big mega rally.

2987
02:09:55.070 --> 02:09:56.760
It's gonna be this huge to do

2988
02:09:56.760 --> 02:09:59.420
and you're like, our taxpayers need not pay for this.

2989
02:09:59.420 --> 02:10:01.597
Here's the bill it's $530,000.

2990
02:10:01.597 --> 02:10:03.840
And as long as you pay it, it's all good.

2991
02:10:03.840 --> 02:10:07.173
And that just led him on fire.

2992
02:10:08.010 --> 02:10:08.843
He got pissed off his whole

2993
02:10:08.843 --> 02:10:09.920
his support is start attacking,

2994
02:10:09.920 --> 02:10:12.770
you gotta wear a Bulletproof vest as mayor for a few days

2995
02:10:12.770 --> 02:10:15.490
because of that, because you've got all these,

2996
02:10:15.490 --> 02:10:17.830
anti-Semitic white supremacist whatever,

2997
02:10:17.830 --> 02:10:22.320
agitators that want to come in and scare you now.

2998
02:10:22.320 --> 02:10:25.460
But in that moment, there wasn't anything spectacular

2999
02:10:25.460 --> 02:10:28.760
about what I did I get tons of credit.

3000
02:10:28.760 --> 02:10:31.510
And I don't know that it was necessarily even deserved.

3001
02:10:32.500 --> 02:10:34.480
Now similarly there have been instances

3002
02:10:34.480 --> 02:10:35.313
where I've been in it,

3003
02:10:35.313 --> 02:10:36.970
which has been in the exact opposite,

3004
02:10:36.970 --> 02:10:39.920
instances that I'm proud of, but you just get ripped apart.

3005
02:10:41.120 --> 02:10:44.880
And so you're, neither is good nor are you as bad

3006
02:10:44.880 --> 02:10:47.680
as things seem to be in that particular moment,

3007
02:10:47.680 --> 02:10:49.383
whatever it is that's the truth.

3008
02:10:50.400 --> 02:10:53.940
Then the skirmish with Trump continued,

3009
02:10:53.940 --> 02:10:56.590
you got into this thing in the midst of

3010
02:10:56.590 --> 02:11:00.793
when the George Floyd protests were starting to peak,

3011
02:11:01.720 --> 02:11:03.950
or he went after you for being weak

3012
02:11:03.950 --> 02:11:05.963
and you defended yourself.

3013
02:11:07.029 --> 02:11:09.153
And that's where the classic,

3014
02:11:10.590 --> 02:11:12.610
when the looting starts the shooting starts tweets

3015
02:11:12.610 --> 02:11:14.120
happened that got flagged by Twitter.

3016
02:11:14.120 --> 02:11:15.610
And that really set in motion,

3017
02:11:15.610 --> 02:11:17.220
his ultimate de platforming,

3018
02:11:17.220 --> 02:11:20.860
like that was kind of a monumental tweet storm

3019
02:11:20.860 --> 02:11:21.783
that went down.

3020
02:11:23.020 --> 02:11:24.850
I mean, that had to been wild for you in that.

3021
02:11:24.850 --> 02:11:26.760
It was wild and I actually didn't,

3022
02:11:26.760 --> 02:11:28.820
I think I was informed of those tweets

3023
02:11:28.820 --> 02:11:32.240
while I was in a press conference at the time,

3024
02:11:32.240 --> 02:11:37.210
which was needless to say one of the worst press conferences

3025
02:11:37.210 --> 02:11:38.620
and performances that I gave largely

3026
02:11:38.620 --> 02:11:40.473
because I was exhausted at that point.

3027
02:11:40.473 --> 02:11:42.373
I think we were three or four days in,

3028
02:11:43.735 --> 02:11:48.068
and the things that Donald Trump spouts off needless

3029
02:11:51.610 --> 02:11:55.370
to say are, I mean, not just irresponsible.

3030
02:11:55.370 --> 02:11:57.870
I mean, my goodness sometimes they were evil

3031
02:11:57.870 --> 02:12:00.330
and thankfully he doesn't have that platform

3032
02:12:00.330 --> 02:12:01.497
at the moment anyway.

3033
02:12:03.490 --> 02:12:08.490
What is the a day in the life look like for you

3034
02:12:08.890 --> 02:12:10.570
is every day different?

3035
02:12:10.570 --> 02:12:12.350
Is there like a walk me through

3036
02:12:12.350 --> 02:12:13.930
like how it all works.

3037
02:12:13.930 --> 02:12:17.323
Every day is different and it depends on the time.

3038
02:12:18.710 --> 02:12:21.180
I mean, when I first took office,

3039
02:12:21.180 --> 02:12:23.910
we were on the verge of having the super bowl

3040
02:12:23.910 --> 02:12:25.940
just a month after I took office,

3041
02:12:25.940 --> 02:12:29.960
we had arguably the largest sporting event

3042
02:12:29.960 --> 02:12:32.120
in the entire world, other than maybe the Olympics

3043
02:12:32.120 --> 02:12:34.120
in the world cup right here in our city.

3044
02:12:35.890 --> 02:12:39.220
And things look like, it was constant briefings,

3045
02:12:39.220 --> 02:12:42.430
getting prepared for safety and security measures.

3046
02:12:42.430 --> 02:12:45.530
It was welcoming people to town,

3047
02:12:45.530 --> 02:12:47.670
welcoming Philadelphia Eagles fans,

3048
02:12:47.670 --> 02:12:50.050
as much as I didn't really care for them.

3049
02:12:50.050 --> 02:12:53.180
It was meeting celebrities the times

3050
02:12:53.180 --> 02:12:57.520
and I mean, that was the bizarre time in and of itself.

3051
02:12:57.520 --> 02:13:00.783
There are days where it's just policy all day long.

3052
02:13:01.840 --> 02:13:06.160
And there are also days where you're just sprinting

3053
02:13:06.160 --> 02:13:07.930
from one event to the next

3054
02:13:07.930 --> 02:13:10.180
that are diametric opposites.

3055
02:13:10.180 --> 02:13:14.510
I mean, you're going from a funeral of a firefighter

3056
02:13:14.510 --> 02:13:16.960
who has died of cancer.

3057
02:13:16.960 --> 02:13:19.530
And then you're wiping away tears in your eyes

3058
02:13:19.530 --> 02:13:22.170
and heading over to do a raw event,

3059
02:13:22.170 --> 02:13:26.810
celebrating Minneapolis and some extraordinary new program

3060
02:13:26.810 --> 02:13:28.710
or business that's coming to town.

3061
02:13:28.710 --> 02:13:32.710
And that's the weird part

3062
02:13:32.710 --> 02:13:35.240
and that's where at times trying

3063
02:13:36.300 --> 02:13:38.540
because it feels fake sometimes

3064
02:13:38.540 --> 02:13:42.260
you're in this sad state at one event

3065
02:13:42.260 --> 02:13:45.850
and you're in the car, maybe sometimes a staff,

3066
02:13:45.850 --> 02:13:49.680
sometimes you were alone and you have to, like,

3067
02:13:49.680 --> 02:13:51.633
as Brogan says, you gotta fake it.

3068
02:13:52.980 --> 02:13:55.820
You got to like intentionally put on this smile

3069
02:13:55.820 --> 02:13:56.653
that is...

3070
02:13:56.653 --> 02:13:59.768
So that's what Brogan's been doing this whole time

3071
02:13:59.768 --> 02:14:01.333
(chuckles) sitting over there.

3072
02:14:04.809 --> 02:14:06.026
You gotta force off

3073
02:14:06.026 --> 02:14:07.710
it was force it you're right.

3074
02:14:07.710 --> 02:14:09.730
Force it is far better than fake it.

3075
02:14:09.730 --> 02:14:11.360
I should've gone with that,

3076
02:14:11.360 --> 02:14:12.750
but as Brogan says you force it,

3077
02:14:12.750 --> 02:14:14.293
and you do have to force it.

3078
02:14:16.260 --> 02:14:19.540
And sometimes you start out forcing it

3079
02:14:19.540 --> 02:14:20.860
for the first couple of minutes

3080
02:14:20.860 --> 02:14:23.833
and then it actually becomes genuine by minute six.

3081
02:14:25.210 --> 02:14:27.527
But that's kind of the reality of these days

3082
02:14:27.527 --> 02:14:31.100
and I mean I try to get runs in

3083
02:14:31.100 --> 02:14:33.300
whether they were really relate

3084
02:14:33.300 --> 02:14:35.410
as much as possible because that grounds me

3085
02:14:35.410 --> 02:14:37.640
and my staff know when I have not gotten in my run,

3086
02:14:37.640 --> 02:14:39.713
my wife certainly is aware.

3087
02:14:41.470 --> 02:14:42.570
You have to run every.

3088
02:14:42.570 --> 02:14:44.449
You got to run, you gotta run.

3089
02:14:44.449 --> 02:14:46.730
I can't imagine how you would like your,

3090
02:14:46.730 --> 02:14:48.340
just your ability to manage

3091
02:14:48.340 --> 02:14:50.780
that level of stress day in day out

3092
02:14:50.780 --> 02:14:54.230
is improved so much from that level of self care.

3093
02:14:54.230 --> 02:14:56.690
When you run, do you have to run with a security detail

3094
02:14:56.690 --> 02:14:58.110
or can you just go out and run?

3095
02:14:58.110 --> 02:15:00.660
Yeah, we had a discussion about that early on.

3096
02:15:00.660 --> 02:15:03.740
And that was like definitively rejected by me

3097
02:15:03.740 --> 02:15:07.080
I was like, this is, no, you know,

3098
02:15:07.080 --> 02:15:09.940
there are times when I want to run with nobody.

3099
02:15:09.940 --> 02:15:12.680
There are times when I just want to zone out,

3100
02:15:12.680 --> 02:15:16.520
not say not talk and just kind of be

3101
02:15:16.520 --> 02:15:19.460
in that moment with my breath.

3102
02:15:19.460 --> 02:15:21.140
It's meditation in a way

3103
02:15:21.140 --> 02:15:26.140
I'm also a very talkative and extroverted person.

3104
02:15:26.880 --> 02:15:27.990
And one of the main things

3105
02:15:27.990 --> 02:15:30.600
that I miss about my running career is not the races

3106
02:15:30.600 --> 02:15:32.170
certainly not the hard workouts.

3107
02:15:32.170 --> 02:15:34.330
It's the comradery it's running with the guys

3108
02:15:34.330 --> 02:15:38.160
So I run routinely with Brogan.

3109
02:15:38.160 --> 02:15:40.793
I mean, that's an extra version explosion.

3110
02:15:42.539 --> 02:15:45.120
Brogan has been giving high fives to every other person

3111
02:15:45.120 --> 02:15:45.953
that he sees.

3112
02:15:45.953 --> 02:15:47.220
He actually can't get anywhere

3113
02:15:47.220 --> 02:15:48.980
cause he has to stop on every street corner

3114
02:15:48.980 --> 02:15:50.463
and strike up a conversation.

3115
02:15:51.610 --> 02:15:55.260
So you're grooming Brogan to be the mayor

3116
02:15:55.260 --> 02:15:57.110
who's gonna follow in your footsteps.

3117
02:15:58.070 --> 02:16:01.630
He's already the mayor of deep cave at,

3118
02:16:01.630 --> 02:16:04.333
or wherever the hell you're living in suburbia.

3119
02:16:07.600 --> 02:16:10.000
I was going for a run with actually

3120
02:16:11.468 --> 02:16:14.410
a New York times reporter who was in town,

3121
02:16:14.410 --> 02:16:17.180
John Agar I haven't met him

3122
02:16:17.180 --> 02:16:20.210
but I know he's, he's here covering everything.

3123
02:16:20.210 --> 02:16:21.980
So do know the story you already?

3124
02:16:21.980 --> 02:16:22.813
No.

3125
02:16:24.270 --> 02:16:27.140
And so I had just largely ditched Brogan

3126
02:16:27.140 --> 02:16:28.784
the night before I was like,

3127
02:16:28.784 --> 02:16:31.279
hey I can not gonna run the next day with you I now,

3128
02:16:31.279 --> 02:16:34.030
because I was gonna get a run with John

3129
02:16:34.030 --> 02:16:37.020
and I show up to pick up John,

3130
02:16:37.020 --> 02:16:38.680
outside of his hotel.

3131
02:16:38.680 --> 02:16:41.500
And we start on the run within a half a block,

3132
02:16:41.500 --> 02:16:42.963
he turns to me and he says,

3133
02:16:44.090 --> 02:16:46.400
so do you know a guy named Brogan?

3134
02:16:46.400 --> 02:16:48.900
(laughs)

3135
02:16:48.900 --> 02:16:51.937
Of course, who doesn't know Brogan

3136
02:16:51.937 --> 02:16:53.839
but of course unbelievable.

3137
02:16:53.839 --> 02:16:55.339
And as it turns out this like,

3138
02:16:56.270 --> 02:16:58.880
tall goofball dude and rollerblades

3139
02:16:58.880 --> 02:17:02.423
had come up to him maybe seven months prior.

3140
02:17:03.270 --> 02:17:07.160
Pink socks and green polka dot hat.

3141
02:17:07.160 --> 02:17:09.080
Struck up a conversation.

3142
02:17:09.080 --> 02:17:10.640
That's pretty much Brogan,

3143
02:17:10.640 --> 02:17:13.210
Brogan the other day we're walking down the street

3144
02:17:13.210 --> 02:17:15.130
and he strikes up a conversation with somebody

3145
02:17:15.130 --> 02:17:17.560
they've been talking for 10 seconds and he goes,

3146
02:17:17.560 --> 02:17:18.790
how do you think our friendships going?

3147
02:17:18.790 --> 02:17:20.230
Are we getting onto the right track?

3148
02:17:20.230 --> 02:17:22.880
Do you feel good about where this is all headed?

3149
02:17:22.880 --> 02:17:24.660
(laughing)

3150
02:17:24.660 --> 02:17:29.660
See no right out of the playbook, anyway, yeah, man.

3151
02:17:31.100 --> 02:17:36.010
I mean, I can't imagine you being the runner

3152
02:17:36.010 --> 02:17:40.050
that you are not making time to run,

3153
02:17:40.050 --> 02:17:42.420
but also understanding that there are gonna be days

3154
02:17:42.420 --> 02:17:44.680
where that's just impossible.

3155
02:17:44.680 --> 02:17:47.410
Some days, some days it is just impossible.

3156
02:17:47.410 --> 02:17:50.260
And I mean, ideal world for me

3157
02:17:50.260 --> 02:17:52.513
is I run five times a week,

3158
02:17:53.440 --> 02:17:57.050
maybe four to seven, eight miles per hour.

3159
02:17:57.050 --> 02:17:59.980
That's kind of like the ideal sweet spot for me

3160
02:17:59.980 --> 02:18:02.160
I don't need to go more than that anymore,

3161
02:18:02.160 --> 02:18:06.370
I don't need to crank any sort of crazy fast pace out,

3162
02:18:06.370 --> 02:18:10.470
but just getting out there and breathing in the movement.

3163
02:18:10.470 --> 02:18:14.800
I mean, it's heaven in so many of those checks.

3164
02:18:14.800 --> 02:18:16.200
Be honest with me,

3165
02:18:16.200 --> 02:18:19.423
what's the casual pace these days?

3166
02:18:21.799 --> 02:18:23.930
I think it probably varies somewhat

3167
02:18:23.930 --> 02:18:25.090
well, first off you go,

3168
02:18:25.090 --> 02:18:26.990
the pace that whoever you're running with,

3169
02:18:26.990 --> 02:18:28.160
if somebody...

3170
02:18:28.160 --> 02:18:31.260
Happy slowing and a half minutes.

3171
02:18:31.260 --> 02:18:34.540
If you person you're running with a six and a half,

3172
02:18:34.540 --> 02:18:36.190
you turn up juice and you hit it,

3173
02:18:37.740 --> 02:18:38.920
I start pretty slow.

3174
02:18:38.920 --> 02:18:42.773
I would say eight and a half minutes a mile.

3175
02:18:44.241 --> 02:18:45.910
This is if I'm on my own.

3176
02:18:45.910 --> 02:18:48.500
And I usually finish the last couple

3177
02:18:48.500 --> 02:18:49.913
of miles around pretty hard,

3178
02:18:52.200 --> 02:18:55.500
Six minutes sometimes even faster to get.

3179
02:18:55.500 --> 02:18:58.400
Yeah just for people that are listening,

3180
02:18:58.400 --> 02:19:02.250
who maybe don't have a frame of reference a two 16 marathon

3181
02:19:02.250 --> 02:19:06.170
is what's that like five, 12 pigs around that.

3182
02:19:06.170 --> 02:19:07.210
Somewhere around that and,

3183
02:19:07.210 --> 02:19:09.763
this is without the fancy shoes.

3184
02:19:11.216 --> 02:19:12.960
(overlapping conversation)

3185
02:19:12.960 --> 02:19:16.610
And I'm just telling people, my PR is 209 because...

3186
02:19:16.610 --> 02:19:19.890
Did you follow Desirees 50K world record the other day?

3187
02:19:19.890 --> 02:19:22.330
I did, I was dead in watch it at the time,

3188
02:19:22.330 --> 02:19:26.270
but I saw that she set the world record

3189
02:19:26.270 --> 02:19:29.150
for the 50 K was the first woman under three hours I believe

3190
02:19:29.150 --> 02:19:32.600
and then I started slugging beer out of her shoe.

3191
02:19:32.600 --> 02:19:35.780
Like, like really consistent 5 47

3192
02:19:35.780 --> 02:19:36.910
it's pretty much across the board.

3193
02:19:36.910 --> 02:19:38.450
Just keep going 5 47, well, I mean,

3194
02:19:38.450 --> 02:19:39.670
she's a metronome that way

3195
02:19:39.670 --> 02:19:40.710
and she was tough on him guess

3196
02:19:40.710 --> 02:19:42.420
when I was out there running with the Hanson shoes,

3197
02:19:42.420 --> 02:19:43.760
she was busted up, she was injured.

3198
02:19:43.760 --> 02:19:45.980
I think she had some sort of stress fracture.

3199
02:19:45.980 --> 02:19:48.113
She was not any sort of like,

3200
02:19:50.310 --> 02:19:52.010
I mean, she was always talented obviously,

3201
02:19:52.010 --> 02:19:54.670
but like she was not crushing it at the time.

3202
02:19:54.670 --> 02:19:57.420
But she's tough, she grinds.

3203
02:19:57.420 --> 02:19:59.130
The harder, the circumstances,

3204
02:19:59.130 --> 02:20:00.670
the more she's able to shine,

3205
02:20:00.670 --> 02:20:03.090
which is why she liked that moment

3206
02:20:03.090 --> 02:20:05.022
at the Boston marathon was so,

3207
02:20:05.022 --> 02:20:07.082
It was so incredible I mean,

3208
02:20:07.082 --> 02:20:08.147
in my favorite moments with Dez

3209
02:20:08.147 --> 02:20:09.340
are not running its partying.

3210
02:20:09.340 --> 02:20:11.700
I mean I remember more moments of partying with her

3211
02:20:11.700 --> 02:20:15.410
and her husband and Brian Sell

3212
02:20:15.410 --> 02:20:17.260
who was out there at the time as well,

3213
02:20:17.260 --> 02:20:19.103
who was the blue collar runner.

3214
02:20:20.310 --> 02:20:22.550
Do you know Josh Cox then too?

3215
02:20:22.550 --> 02:20:23.730
I've met Josh Cox,

3216
02:20:23.730 --> 02:20:26.260
I can't say I know him really well though.

3217
02:20:26.260 --> 02:20:28.890
Do you still follow running and marathoning

3218
02:20:28.890 --> 02:20:29.760
and all that pretty closely?

3219
02:20:29.760 --> 02:20:30.593
Yeah I do?

3220
02:20:32.830 --> 02:20:36.410
No, it's there's I definitely stay in touch with those

3221
02:20:36.410 --> 02:20:39.080
that are still involved in the sport.

3222
02:20:39.080 --> 02:20:41.810
I mean it's such a beautiful sport to follow.

3223
02:20:41.810 --> 02:20:44.030
What else is part of your routine?

3224
02:20:44.030 --> 02:20:45.193
Are you a meditator?

3225
02:20:47.405 --> 02:20:51.883
I'm not, I mean I'm not a meditator in traditional form,

3226
02:20:53.830 --> 02:20:55.870
but I guess there are probably many forms of Medicaid.

3227
02:20:55.870 --> 02:20:57.750
I mean, I'm not on the ground,

3228
02:20:57.750 --> 02:21:02.080
with my legs crossed and I don't do that.

3229
02:21:02.080 --> 02:21:06.710
I've tried it my mind wanders too much.

3230
02:21:06.710 --> 02:21:09.280
I know that those that are more experienced meditators

3231
02:21:09.280 --> 02:21:10.830
would probably say that's okay.

3232
02:21:12.600 --> 02:21:17.600
I would get frustrated I use breath and mantra,

3233
02:21:20.070 --> 02:21:22.070
which always has worked for me.

3234
02:21:22.070 --> 02:21:27.030
It's repetition of a phrase to sort of set my mind

3235
02:21:27.030 --> 02:21:28.403
and ground myself.

3236
02:21:30.380 --> 02:21:31.860
And I mean, my wife just calls it,

3237
02:21:31.860 --> 02:21:34.710
talking to myself, but that's what I'm doing.

3238
02:21:34.710 --> 02:21:37.150
Yeah, well, I would consider that meditation.

3239
02:21:37.150 --> 02:21:39.010
So there you go you can say that you're a meditator.

3240
02:21:39.010 --> 02:21:41.910
I am a meditator, I will say the next time someone asks.

3241
02:21:42.960 --> 02:21:44.290
Where's this all going for you?

3242
02:21:44.290 --> 02:21:46.533
I mean, do you imagine yourself,

3243
02:21:47.770 --> 02:21:50.190
being mayor here and like, what's next,

3244
02:21:50.190 --> 02:21:51.670
do you think about the future?

3245
02:21:51.670 --> 02:21:54.850
What's your vision for your own career and your life?

3246
02:21:54.850 --> 02:21:56.090
I think everybody thinks about

3247
02:21:56.090 --> 02:21:58.840
what their future is and what it holds.

3248
02:21:58.840 --> 02:22:02.697
I also think that my vision for my future

3249
02:22:02.697 --> 02:22:04.610
has probably changed dramatically

3250
02:22:04.610 --> 02:22:06.123
in the last year and a half.

3251
02:22:08.950 --> 02:22:13.343
If you'd ask me two years ago,

3252
02:22:14.680 --> 02:22:16.600
I think I would have been pretty enthusiastic

3253
02:22:16.600 --> 02:22:18.953
about a longer career in politics.

3254
02:22:22.240 --> 02:22:23.710
if you ask me right now,

3255
02:22:23.710 --> 02:22:26.620
I feel a deep seated responsibility

3256
02:22:26.620 --> 02:22:27.780
and I love being mayor,

3257
02:22:27.780 --> 02:22:29.300
not withstanding everything else.

3258
02:22:29.300 --> 02:22:31.610
I still do love this job.

3259
02:22:31.610 --> 02:22:33.100
Do I love it on a daily basis?

3260
02:22:33.100 --> 02:22:35.490
No, You know, I don't,

3261
02:22:35.490 --> 02:22:39.283
but at the end of the day there's this feeling

3262
02:22:41.020 --> 02:22:43.110
that you can kind of work hand in hand with community,

3263
02:22:43.110 --> 02:22:44.600
towards the betterment of people.

3264
02:22:44.600 --> 02:22:45.913
And like, I love that.

3265
02:22:47.242 --> 02:22:50.100
And so I love mayor, I wanna be mayor,

3266
02:22:50.100 --> 02:22:52.180
but do I want to do anything beyond that?

3267
02:22:52.180 --> 02:22:53.330
Honestly, I don't know.

3268
02:22:55.040 --> 02:22:57.590
If standing here today, I would say absolutely not.

3269
02:22:58.450 --> 02:23:00.030
I have no interest in being in Congress

3270
02:23:00.030 --> 02:23:01.756
for a number of reasons.

3271
02:23:01.756 --> 02:23:04.310
One, I mean with the exception of recently,

3272
02:23:04.310 --> 02:23:06.413
traditionally they haven't gotten a lot done,

3273
02:23:08.690 --> 02:23:11.790
but also I don't want to be flying back

3274
02:23:11.790 --> 02:23:13.670
and forth for selfish reasons.

3275
02:23:13.670 --> 02:23:14.610
I don't want to be flying back

3276
02:23:14.610 --> 02:23:16.190
and forth between DC and Minnesota.

3277
02:23:16.190 --> 02:23:21.190
I got a family, I have a wonderful wife, Sarah,

3278
02:23:21.310 --> 02:23:22.300
we have a baby.

3279
02:23:22.300 --> 02:23:23.650
I want to be with my family

3280
02:23:24.980 --> 02:23:27.380
rather than flying back and forth without them.

3281
02:23:27.380 --> 02:23:28.550
The campaign piece, I mean,

3282
02:23:28.550 --> 02:23:31.170
typically around now is where

3283
02:23:31.170 --> 02:23:33.910
that would kind of kick into high gear.

3284
02:23:33.910 --> 02:23:37.010
But I suspect that the pressures of the job

3285
02:23:37.010 --> 02:23:40.700
or making that not that kind of goes out the window.

3286
02:23:40.700 --> 02:23:41.653
Campaign has not,

3287
02:23:42.723 --> 02:23:46.430
is barely happened at all over these last several months

3288
02:23:46.430 --> 02:23:48.900
and I expect the next several months to come.

3289
02:23:48.900 --> 02:23:53.900
It's just such a lesser priority

3290
02:23:54.480 --> 02:23:57.010
than keeping the city safe through this time

3291
02:23:57.010 --> 02:23:58.810
and making sure that people are okay.

3292
02:23:58.810 --> 02:24:03.810
And so no way 98% of my time is really devoted to that.

3293
02:24:05.310 --> 02:24:07.970
Well, I feel like setting aside

3294
02:24:07.970 --> 02:24:11.070
the kind of urgency of what this city

3295
02:24:11.070 --> 02:24:12.980
is enduring at the moment,

3296
02:24:12.980 --> 02:24:17.050
you being the accomplished elite endurance athlete

3297
02:24:17.050 --> 02:24:17.883
that you are,

3298
02:24:17.883 --> 02:24:22.883
and the kind of robustness of this city

3299
02:24:23.110 --> 02:24:26.420
with respect to its parks and its bike ways

3300
02:24:26.420 --> 02:24:27.970
and everything that it has to offer

3301
02:24:27.970 --> 02:24:31.840
in terms of outside activity and adventure,

3302
02:24:31.840 --> 02:24:33.660
puts you in this position to be kind

3303
02:24:33.660 --> 02:24:35.770
of like the wellness mayor, like,

3304
02:24:35.770 --> 02:24:37.560
because you live this lifestyle

3305
02:24:37.560 --> 02:24:40.970
and you're in a city that's so conducive

3306
02:24:40.970 --> 02:24:44.480
to these kinds of participation, oriented endeavors,

3307
02:24:44.480 --> 02:24:49.480
that that feels like a leadership role that suits you well,

3308
02:24:49.510 --> 02:24:51.600
does that resonate for you?

3309
02:24:51.600 --> 02:24:52.703
Of course it does.

3310
02:24:52.703 --> 02:24:54.240
It's like if everything was calm

3311
02:24:54.240 --> 02:24:55.540
and there was nothing going on here,

3312
02:24:55.540 --> 02:24:57.350
maybe you could focus on that now

3313
02:24:57.350 --> 02:24:58.703
is not the moment for that.

3314
02:24:59.930 --> 02:25:02.620
Used to focus on that all the time.

3315
02:25:02.620 --> 02:25:03.920
I've got this incredible story

3316
02:25:03.920 --> 02:25:05.090
that I got to tell you

3317
02:25:05.090 --> 02:25:08.630
about Abdi Bile have you heard of this guy?

3318
02:25:08.630 --> 02:25:09.620
Yes.

3319
02:25:09.620 --> 02:25:14.620
So Abdi Bile somali man won the world championships

3320
02:25:16.400 --> 02:25:17.560
for the 1500 meters.

3321
02:25:17.560 --> 02:25:20.320
He was the best miler in the entire world back

3322
02:25:20.320 --> 02:25:24.723
in 89, 88, 90 in that timeframe.

3323
02:25:26.170 --> 02:25:27.640
And he went to George Mason University,

3324
02:25:27.640 --> 02:25:30.550
which was right down the street from where I lived.

3325
02:25:30.550 --> 02:25:32.800
And when I was like a ten-year-old,

3326
02:25:32.800 --> 02:25:37.280
I used to take the bus over to George Mason University

3327
02:25:37.280 --> 02:25:42.000
to try to warm up with some of these guys

3328
02:25:42.000 --> 02:25:43.030
that were there at the time.

3329
02:25:43.030 --> 02:25:44.210
I mean, Abdi Bile was there,

3330
02:25:44.210 --> 02:25:46.933
Julius H Shawn was there a little after that,

3331
02:25:46.933 --> 02:25:48.483
but Abdi was like my hero.

3332
02:25:49.420 --> 02:25:53.240
And there was this one day where I was running alone,

3333
02:25:53.240 --> 02:25:56.410
like 10 years old and I see this tall,

3334
02:25:56.410 --> 02:25:59.750
dark lanky figure running towards me

3335
02:25:59.750 --> 02:26:01.470
and he gets close I realized, oh my God.

3336
02:26:01.470 --> 02:26:05.370
It's like, it's the world champion Abdi Bile

3337
02:26:05.370 --> 02:26:06.450
and I thought to myself,

3338
02:26:06.450 --> 02:26:08.317
I'll never forgive myself if I don't turn around

3339
02:26:08.317 --> 02:26:09.940
and run with them.

3340
02:26:09.940 --> 02:26:11.690
So I did, I turned around,

3341
02:26:11.690 --> 02:26:12.680
I started running with them

3342
02:26:12.680 --> 02:26:15.960
and I was so excited for this moment

3343
02:26:15.960 --> 02:26:18.170
that I probably quickened my pace a little bit.

3344
02:26:18.170 --> 02:26:21.960
And I was one stepping him and I member

3345
02:26:21.960 --> 02:26:25.970
he put his hand on my shoulder and he said to me,

3346
02:26:25.970 --> 02:26:29.380
Jacob is not important how fast you go

3347
02:26:29.380 --> 02:26:32.720
it is only important that you go with purpose.

3348
02:26:32.720 --> 02:26:35.770
And at the time I thought this was like some Somali proverb

3349
02:26:35.770 --> 02:26:38.240
or famous saying that he was just repeating.

3350
02:26:38.240 --> 02:26:42.070
But now 30 years later, having getting to know him

3351
02:26:42.070 --> 02:26:43.350
is just how the guy talks now.

3352
02:26:43.350 --> 02:26:45.189
He was the crazy thing.

3353
02:26:45.189 --> 02:26:46.720
So, and that moment when I was running with them,

3354
02:26:46.720 --> 02:26:49.420
I don't think I saw Abdi Bile in person

3355
02:26:49.420 --> 02:26:51.053
for the next 25 years,

3356
02:26:52.500 --> 02:26:55.320
fast forward 25 years I'm the mayor of Minneapolis,

3357
02:26:55.320 --> 02:26:57.610
this is a couple of years ago.

3358
02:26:57.610 --> 02:27:02.610
I get a message on Facebook or text or something like that

3359
